    "solution": "[Textbook misprint: printed as \\(4\\cos x\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) + \\cos^2\\left(\\pi - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\cos 3x\\), which is FALSE — the second term is a CONSTANT (\\(\\cos^2 120^\\circ = \\tfrac14\\)), and the statement fails at every one of 7 sample angles. Three corruptions at once: a \\(+\\) where a product belongs, \\(\\cos^2\\) for \\(\\cos\\), and \\(\\pi\\) for \\(x\\). The intended identity \\(4\\cos x\\cos\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\cos\\left(x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = \\cos 3x\\) holds at all 7, and is what is proved above.]"
